    "result_text": "All 33 tests pass and the build succeeds, including the new `Cp22Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` field following the existing `namesakeCount` pattern (a moment-in-time value captured at owner creation and read back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `bulkSignupWarning` read-only boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (model) \u2014 added a `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field mapped to a `bulk_signup_warning` column, with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column to the `owners` table.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `assignBulkSignupWarning(owner)` to the `addOwner` create pipeline. It counts existing owners already carrying this owner's (business-day-adjusted) `registrationDate` and sets the flag `true` when that count exceeds 80 (`> 80`), otherwise `false` \u2014 reusing the same per-day accumulation path as the existing `rejectDailyLimit` rule.\n\n5. The MapStruct `OwnerMapper` auto-maps the field by name (`Owner.bulkSignupWarning` \u2192 `OwnerDto.bulkSignupWarning`), so no mapper change was needed.\n\nThe spec's \"more than 80\" is implemented strictly as `count > 80` (i.e., the 82nd+ owner of a day, when 81+ already exist). The fresh-create case yields `false`, matching the test.",
